# The `kernel-x64` producer: the x64 kernel package, `mos-kernel-x64`.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no command substitution, no
# expansion. Safe to source from bash and to parse with grep/sed from anything
# else, and checked for that shape by build-env/deb/build.sh before it is
# sourced. build-env/deb/README.md documents every field.
#
#   bash build-env/deb/build.sh --producer kernel-x64 --arch amd64
#
# The BSP output arrives through PREPARE below.

# One package, and it is SEPARATE from mos-board-x64 rather than folded into
# it. The two answer different questions -- which kernel this board runs, and
# how this board's partitions and bootloader are configured -- and they move
# for different reasons: a kernel bump changes versions.env and the recorded
# config, a layout change changes board.env. Keeping them apart is also what
# makes the change visible where it is measured: /usr/share/mos/manifest.tsv
# and rootfs-report.txt list installed size per package, and the whole point of
# PLAN-073 was that one of those rows was 108 MB.
PACKAGES="mos-kernel-x64"

# amd64 only, and not `all`. The payload is an x86_64 kernel image and its
# modules; an `all` archive would declare a package the cx3576 image could
# install.
ARCHES="amd64"

# NO VERSION_FROM, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
#
# This producer repacks upstream software, which is the case VERSION_FROM
# exists for -- it would make the archive 6.12.107+git<commit>-1 instead of
# 0.1.0+git<commit>-1. What stops it is the direction of the dependency:
# mos-board-x64 must Depend on this package at its exact pool version, and the
# only tokens build-env/deb/pack.sh renders are @VERSION@ (the depending
# package's own) and @SYSTEM_VERSION@ (mos-system's). There is no token for a
# third package's version, and adding one would be new packaging machinery for
# a single edge.
#
# The kernel version is not lost by that choice. It is pinned in
# boards/x64/bsp/kernel/versions.env, it is the release string in every payload
# path (/boot/vmlinuz-<release>, /usr/lib/modules/<release>/), the Dockerfile
# asserts the two agree, and the package description states it.

# The host-side step. The BSP output is selected by BOARD_DIR -- the same
# override rootfs/build.sh and the cx3576 producer accept -- and a build
# context in this file is a fixed repository-relative path, because these lines
# never expand a variable. A directory chosen at run time therefore cannot be
# named below. The hook also refuses a missing kernel before any container has
# been started.
PREPARE="stage.sh"

# stage.sh honours MOS_DEB_PREFLIGHT=1, so build-env/deb/preflight.sh can
# report a missing kernel together with every other producer's missing input
# before `make os-debs` starts a container. Without it, a fresh worktree learns
# that it has no x64 kernel only when this producer's turn comes.
PREFLIGHT="1"

# This producer's own committed files. The kernel, its modules and the config
# it was built from are NOT here: they are gitignored BSP output and arrive as
# `bin` from PREPARE. boards/x64/bsp/out reaches hundreds of megabytes, and a
# context path that does not exist until a build has run has no place in a file
# that describes the tree.
BUILD_CONTEXTS="kerneldeb=boards/x64/deb/kernel-x64"

# Nothing here is enabled at multi-user.target. The payload is a kernel image,
# its modules and the config that describes them -- read by the bootloader and
# by modprobe, never started by systemd.
ENABLEMENT="mos-kernel-x64=0"
